 *
 * KERMIT CARRIES A SECOND HONESTY BURDEN STANTON DOES NOT. Winkler County is
 * NOT among the nine counties in COUNTIES_SERVED — it is outside the declared
 * territory as well as off the route. The page must say so plainly rather than
 * letting a reader infer normal coverage from the fact that a page exists.
 *
 * Same rules as Stanton: framing above the fold; never imply a call history or
 * an existing customer; name what does not fit and who to ring instead; and if
 * he ever runs Winkler County properly, this gets rewritten UP rather than
 * drifting upward a sentence at a time.
 * ############################################################################
 *
 * VERIFIED facts used here:
 *  - Ordinance: Kermit Code of Ordinances ch. 7 (Health and Sanitation),
 *    art. 7.02 "Weeds, Rubbish, Brush, and Other Unsanitary Matter"
 *    (eCode360), §§ 7.02.001–7.02.013.
 *      · 7.02.004 — TEN INCHES. Strictest on this route except Lubbock's
 *        eight. Applies to owners, tenants, lessees, renters and agents.
 *      · 7.02.008 — TEN DAYS to correct. Service by personal delivery, by
 *        mail (DEEMED RECEIVED FIVE DAYS after mailing), by newspaper
 *        publication twice within ten consecutive days, by posting on the
 *        door, or by posting on a stake on the property. A repeat violator
 *        cited within 24 months may be issued a citation with no new notice.
 *      · 7.02.009 — subsequent violations of the same nature within a
 *        TWENTY-FOUR MONTH period may be corrected without further notice.
 *        Every other town on this route runs a TWELVE-month window.
 *      · 7.02.010 — city does the work, costs are a charge AND a personal
 *        liability; city manager assesses against the real estate and may
 *        file a lien statement with the county clerk; privileged lien at
 *        TEN PERCENT annual interest.
 *      · 7.02.011 — vegetation over FORTY-EIGHT INCHES posing immediate
 *        danger may be abated with NO ADVANCE NOTICE; notice follows within
 *        ten days; owner may request an administrative hearing within 30
 *        days, to be held within 20 days of the request.
 *      · 7.02.012 injunctive relief and civil actions; 7.02.013 penalty.
 *  - Winkler County IS on the imported fire ant regulated-areas list under
 *    7 C.F.R. §301.81-3, which 4 TAC §19.101 adopts by reference. Read
 *    directly off the APHIS list (last updated 27 October 2022) Aug 2026.
 *    Gaines, Yoakum, Andrews, Dawson and Terry are NOT on it.
 *  - Census: 2020 population 6,267; July 2025 estimate 6,019. Land area
 *    2.52 sq mi. Housing units 2,609 and households 2,193 at the 2020
 *    census. Owner-occupied rate 84.5% (ACS 2020–2024) — the HIGHEST of any
 *    town on this route. Median owner-occupied value $151,600; median
 *    household income $81,170; 2.66 persons per household.
 *  - Winkler County (TSHA): 840 sq mi; elevation 2,671–3,193 ft; annual
 *    precipitation averages TWELVE INCHES — the driest county on this route;
 *    January lows around 28°F, July highs around 97°F; 220-day growing
 *    season. Dark-brown to reddish-brown sands and sandy loams. A diagonal
 *    SAND DUNE BELT crosses the central county, dunes 30–40 ft above the
 *    surrounding terrain. Creosotebush, cacti, scrub, scattered willows and
 *    wild plums.
 *  - History (TSHA): supply centre for scattered ranches; named for Kermit
 *    Roosevelt, son of Theodore Roosevelt, who hunted antelope at the T Bar
 *    Ranch shortly before the naming. County seat since the county organised
 *    in 1910; first school and post office the same year. Oil found in the
 *    Hendrick field 16 July 1926 (Hendrick No. 1). Population 1,000 in 1927,
 *    1,500 by 1929; Texas–New Mexico Railway arrived 4 March 1929; 2,700
 *    residents and 180 businesses by the end of the 1930s. Incorporated
 *    15 February 1938. Census: 1940 2,584 · 1950 6,912 · 1960 10,465 ·
 *    1970 7,884 · 1980 8,015 · 1990 6,875 · 2000 5,714. County population
 *    peaked at 13,652 in 1960. The last working wooden derrick in the
 *    Permian Basin was moved to Pioneer Park in Kermit in 1966.
 *  - NO pest control company holds a street address in Kermit. Directory
 *    results are Odessa, Monahans, Pecos, Midland, and national chains
 *    serving from elsewhere.
 *
 * NOT FOUND — MUST NOT BE STATED:
 *  - Named neighbourhoods or subdivisions in Kermit. D-09 named none.
 *  - Exact road mileage Kermit→Odessa or Kermit→Monahans.
 *  - Median year structure built.
 *  - Any named employer.
 *  - Any claim about the aquifer or water quality. Out of scope and not ours.
 */
